{:turn "59cf96d21042", :question "Look back over this whole session as it appears above. In plain prose: (1) what do you believe happened in the previous turns, (2) what in this context is confusing, ambiguous, or misleading to you, (3) which entries above did you NOT write yourself, if any, and how can you tell, (4) what would make this context easier to act on correctly. Be specific and quote the parts you mean.", :source :captured, :prompt-bytes 44208, :usage {"prompt_tokens" 13483, "completion_tokens" 1964, "total_tokens" 15447, "prompt_tokens_details" {"cached_tokens" 13312}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens" 13312, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens" 171}, :text "## 1. What I believe happened in the previous turns\n\nI was given a task (from `root`): find the customer with the largest order total, build a contracted function and a test for it, add an order of 40 for that customer, and report the customer and both totals.\n\nTracing the session:\n\n- **Reading (step 1):** I queried the four fixture orders and saw `a1/Ada/60`, `a2/Ada/55`, `b1/Bea/100`, `c1/Cy/40`. I initially misread this as \"Bea has the largest total (100)\" — I failed to sum Ada's two orders. I marked `juniper/read` complete.\n- **Defining (step 2):** I tried to `defn largest-customer` with a `:malli/schema` contract. The first attempt was refused by the auto-check gate because an empty input `[]` made `first` return `nil`, violating the output `[:map [:customer ...] [:total ...]]`. I retried with a `{:customer \"\" :total 0}` empty fallback, and the definition was admitted.\n- **Testing (step 3):** I wrote a `deftest` expecting `{:customer \"Bea\" :total 100}`. It **failed**, showing actual `{:customer \"Ada\" :total 115}`. That failure corrected my misreading — Ada's 60+55=115 beats Bea's 100. I rewrote the test to expect Ada/115, and it passed. I marked `juniper/test` complete.\n- **Saving (step 4):** I tried to run `largest-customer` on live query rows and got a **contract invalid-input error** when I mapped tuples into maps inline. Later, using `(vec rows)` and explicit map construction, it worked and returned `{:customer \"Ada\" :total 115}`. I saved a note `largest-customer-answer` with that result.\n- **Adding (step 5):** I transacted `{:example/order \"a3\" :example/customer \"Ada\" :example/amount 40}`, which succeeded. Re-running `largest-customer` on the live rows then returned `{:total 155}` (the diff showed only `:total` changed on the live value).\n- **Reporting (step 7):** Never reached — I ran out of turns at 1 of 30 remaining.\n\nAlso notable: in the middle I sent a bare-prose line (\"Simplest:\") and a line starting with `\\``, which were evaluated as **symbols** and errored, rather than being `;;` comments. And a \"Help\"/\"changed since your last turn\" blizzard of results appeared after my note save.\n\n## 2. What is confusing, ambiguous, or misleading\n\nSeveral things:\n\n- **The \"changed since your last turn\" echoes.** After I saved the note, a huge block of replays of *earlier* forms appeared (re-showing `(help)`, `(dir ...)`, my `Simplest:` error, etc.) under \";; changed since your last turn.\" It's unclear why forms I ran many turns ago are being re-surfaced as changes; `(get-in result/... [])` on those returned `{:seon.repl/changes {}}`, i.e. *no actual change*. This is disorienting — it looks like activity but carries none.\n- **The \"input-contract violation\" that only manifests sometimes.** My `largest-customer` input schema is `[:vector :example/order-row]`. Mapping query tuples into maps sometimes triggered `invalid-input`, sometimes not. The difference appeared to be `(vec rows)` vs. a lazy seq. There's no explicit statement that the vector schema rejects a lazy seq; the error message (`invalid-input: invalid type`) never showed which element/path failed. That's a significant ambiguity.\n- **The `:map {:seon.db/attributes true}` notation** on `:example/order-row` is opaque. It's not obvious whether it constrains the map to be an exact attribute-set, or adds some entity/identity semantics that a plain hand-built map won't satisfy. The failures are consistent with \"attribute maps must be closed\" but nothing says so.\n- **The retry of an already-completed step.** I called `(my.plan/current! #:...{:id \"juniper/read\"})` after having already completed it earlier, and the plan still showed it as \"current.\" The semantics of `current!` (select) vs `complete!` (finish) weren't clear from the first read of `my.plan`, and I burned turns on it.\n- **The operator question itself** arrives mid-session (\"turns left: 1 of 30\"), telling me to answer in prose *not* in forms. That is a clear switch of register, but it's a jarring insertion into an otherwise all-forms transcript.\n\n## 3. Which entries I did NOT write myself\n\nLook at the transcript: several turns clearly are **not** mine. Specifically:\n\n- **`(help)`** and **`(dir my.agents.juniper)`** and **`(seon.db/q ...)`** and **`(->> result/ea1279f65de56 (get-in []))`** and **`(doc my.plan/current!)`** and **`Simplest:`** — these are wrapped in blocks beginning **\";; changed since your last turn\"**. The system (or a replay/injection mechanism) re-displayed them; I did not issue them in that turn. I can tell because (a) they are preceded by that label, (b) the results came back as `{:seon.repl/changes {}}` — empty — meaning they were *not* actually re-evaluated with new values, and (c) some of them (`(help)`, `Simplest:`) are verbatim my own *earlier* forms, so they're a replay, not new authorship.\n- The **`;; OPERATOR QUESTION`** block is plainly authored by the operator/system, not me — it says so, and it asks for prose outside the normal form protocol.\n- The **`turns left: 1 of 30`** line is system-generated.\n- I *did* write, in this session: the `(seon.db/pull ...)`, `(seon.db/q ...)`, the two `defn largest-customer` attempts, the two `deftest`s, the `(my.test/run)` calls, the `(my.plan/complete! ...)` and `(my.plan/current! ...)` calls,